    "body": "A 74-year-old truck driver from Reno, Nevada, died after his tractor-trailer left Interstate 84 and overturned east of Boise, Idaho, early Monday.\nThe single-vehicle crash occurred at approximately 3:08 a.m. Oct. 12 near milepost 62 in Ada County, along the eastbound lanes of I-84, according to Idaho State Police.\nAuthorities said the driver was operating a 2024 International semi-truck pulling a trailer when the vehicle left the roadway, traveled along an embankment for a short distance and overturned.\nThe driver was transported by ground ambulance to a local hospital, where he later died from his injuries.\nAuthorities said the driver was not wearing a seat belt at the time of the crash.\nThe driver’s identity had not been released in the initial police report.\nThe crash blocked the right eastbound lane of I-84 for approximately three hours while emergency crews responded and investigators examined the scene.\nAuthorities have not disclosed what caused the truck to leave the roadway. The incident remains under investigation.\nInterstate 84 is a major east-west freight corridor connecting Idaho with Oregon and Utah, serving commercial trucking operations throughout the Pacific Northwest and Intermountain West.\nFatal truck crashes remain a national safety concern\nFederal crash data show thousands of fatal crashes involving large commercial trucks each year, although complete figures for January through September 2026 are not yet available.\nThe Federal Motor Carrier Safety Administration ’s preliminary Motor Carrier Management Information System data, updated Aug. 28, recorded 1,607 fatal crashes involving large trucks nationwide in 2026, resulting in 1,768 deaths.\nThe figures represent crashes reported through the agency’s available data snapshot, not a complete January-through-September tally.\nFor comparison, FMCSA’s database lists 3,784 fatal large-truck crashes and 4,209 fatalities for calendar year 2025. Those figures also remain provisional and subject to revision.\nThe National Highway Traffic Safety Administration reported that 5,340 people died in crashes involving large trucks in 2024, down 2.5% from 2023. About 70% of those killed were occupants of other vehicles.\nIn Idaho, preliminary figures released Sept. 11 by the Idaho Transportation Department showed 150 traffic deaths statewide in 2026, compared with 174 during the corresponding period in 2025. Those totals include all vehicle types, not just commercial trucks.\nThe department identified failure to wear a seat belt, failure to maintain a lane, impairment, inattention and speeding among the leading contributing circumstances in fatal crashes statewide.\nInvestigators in Idaho have not determined whether any of those factors, other than the driver’s reported lack of a seat belt, played a role in Monday’s crash.\nWhy it matters: The fatal rollover highlights the risks commercial drivers face in single-vehicle crashes and the importance of seat belt use.\nMore FreightWaves articles by Noi Mahoney:\nMexico trucker shortages, visa restrictions squeeze cross-border capacity\nTexas trucking firm sued over age limits on drivers\nNew Jersey CDL school lawsuit questions evidence behind FMCSA shutdown\nUpcoming FreightWaves Events\nCompliance\nBrokerage Compliance Symposium\nThe day before F3. Every compliance issue you face - fraud exposure, carrier liability, FMCSA rules, cargo theft, insurance gaps - navigated by attorneys and operators defining best practices in a changing industry.\nOctober 26, 2026\nThe Signal at Chattanooga Choo Choo • Chattanooga, TN\nRegister Now\nAwards\nF3 Awards Dinner\nThe night before F3. FreightTech100 companies honored. FreightTech 25 and Shipper of Choice winners revealed live. Cocktail reception into dinner and live music - 300 industry leaders in one purpose-built room.\nOctober 26, 2026\nThe Signal at Chattanooga Choo Choo • Chattanooga, TN\nRegister Now\nFreightTech\nF3: Future of Freight Festival\nIndustry-defining keynotes, rapid-fire technology demos, and industry leaders networking in experiences across Chattanooga - plus the inaugural F3 Awards Dinner featuring the FreightTech and Shipper of Choice reveals.\nOctober 27, 2026 – October 28, 2026\nThe Signal at Chattanooga Choo Choo • Chattanooga, TN\nRegister Now\nCompliance Brokerage Compliance Symposium Oct 26 • The Signal at Chattanooga Choo Choo • Chattanooga, TN\nThe day before F3. Every compliance issue you face - fraud exposure, carrier liability, FMCSA rules, cargo theft, insurance gaps - navigated by attorneys and operators defining best practices in a changing industry.\nThe Signal at Chattanooga Choo Choo • Chattanooga, TN Register Now\nAwards F3 Awards Dinner Oct 26 • The Signal at Chattanooga Choo Choo • Chattanooga, TN\nThe night before F3. FreightTech100 companies honored. FreightTech 25 and Shipper of Choice winners revealed live. Cocktail reception into dinner and live music - 300 industry leaders in one purpose-built room.\nThe Signal at Chattanooga Choo Choo • Chattanooga, TN Register Now\nFreightTech F3: Future of Freight Festival Oct 27 – Oct 28 • The Signal at Chattanooga Choo Choo • Chattanooga, TN\nIndustry-defining keynotes, rapid-fire technology demos, and industry leaders networking in experiences across Chattanooga - plus the inaugural F3 Awards Dinner featuring the FreightTech and Shipper of Choice reveals.\nThe Signal at Chattanooga Choo Choo • Chattanooga, TN Register Now",
